# fixedbugs, 2026-09-16 (defect 053, found by panel 159's completeness critic
# and by no seat, while auditing whether the sitting's R3 sentence about
# `main`'s signature was one the compiler actually checks).
#
# SYMPTOM: `function main(n: i64)` passed `heroes check` at exit 0 and `heroes
# run` exited 2 with `internal error: compiling the generated C failed:
# too few arguments to function call, single argument 'h0_n' was not
# specified`. A program the checker had passed, refused by clang, reported in
# clang's voice.
#
# CAUSE: `selfhost/check/decls.hero` guarded `main`'s RESULT — that guard is
# panel 035's, bought when `main() -> i64?` returning a failure printed nothing
# and exited 0 — and nothing read its PARAMETERS. The emitted `int main` calls
# `h_<module>_main()` with no arguments, so a declared parameter has nothing
# that could ever pass it a value. The only rule that caught any of these was
# `unused_binding`, which fires on an unread parameter of ANY function and says
# nothing about `main`: read the parameter, as here, and the program reached
# the backend.
#
# FIX: the parameter half of the same guard, beside the result half, so the two
# sentences about `main`'s signature are enforced in one place.
#
# The legal neighbours are the other three signatures: a plain `main`, an
# explicit `-> ()` (which passes by construction, because the result guard asks
# `result != unit`), and any other function, which takes parameters freely.

function helper(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function main(n: i64)  #~ main_parameters
    print(f"{helper(n)}")
